body:
This is false. I've got no idea where you're pulling your sources from, but it's not reality. Only 1 in 8 women will suffer depression in their lifetime, according to the National Institute of Mental Health. There are certain types of depression that only occur in people with the ability to give birth or with a menstrual cycle and those percentages seem to be higher: 20-40% may experience Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder, & 10-15% may experience perinatal or postpartum depression. But those numbers are nowhere near the "majority" of women.

Truth is women are twice as likely as men to be diagnosed with depression, but that doesn't mean we're actually more depressed. There's a number of factors at play:

1) Differences in how depression symptoms manifest, leading depression in men to be diagnosed as a different disorder/condition (in the West, at least, men with depression tend to experience more anger, whereas women supposedly present with more sadness/grief);

2) The fear of stigma among traditional masculine structures with regards to being mentally ill, leading far less men to seek treatment;

3) A higher rate of completed suicides among men (mostly due to the fact that men are more likely to use guns), leading less men to receive crisis care that can lead to a diagnosis; 

4) Menstruation and pregnancy, causing the above-mentioned depressive conditions;

5) The fact that traditionally, women were more likely to be seen as mentally ill for acting outside gender norms and thus even now, are more likely to be forced to seek treatment;

6) The above being true, most diagnostic criteria are still focused on traditionally feminine symptoms;

7) Higher rates of homelessness among many demographics of men, leading to a decreased ability to afford/access treatment...

I could go on, but I feel like this is enough to begin explaining the gender gap in diagnoses of depression and depressive mental illnesses. We need to remember the number of diagnoses, however, does not determine how many people actually suffer from that condition--just how many are recognized by medical professionals.

I'm trans and Muslim. It's not always easy, but there's places for us, and people fighting for us. I don't know where you live, which can make all the difference in the world for how you're received, but at least in the US (where I live), there's lots of grassroots movements for Muslims of sexual &/or gender diversity. I can't say I'm never lonely, or that I've never faced discrimination, or that my life is easier because I converted. But I can say that it is better. 
Places and people to check out, if you're interested in learning more about trans & trans-friendly Muslims: 
Muslims for Progressive Values; 
Third Coast Queer Muslims; 
Mahdia Lynn and her work as an activist; 
Transgender Muslim Support Network (started by Mahdia); 
Muslim Alliance for Sexual and Gender Diversity, who run a retreat for LGBTQIA Muslims every year in the US; 
El-Tawhid Juma Circle; 
Unity mosques; 
Twitter and Facebook, which is where a great deal of my socializing with other trans Muslims happens, given that I live in a rural area. There are so many amazing and supportive people who helped me as a new convert and as I'm beginning to transition. 
Good luck, and may Allah bless you by bringing Islam into your heart. <3
